    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\nThe number of trailing zeroes in $n!$ is determined by the minimum of the number of factors of 2 and 5 in its prime factorization, as trailing zeroes come from factors of 10. Since there are typically more factors of 2 than 5, the number of trailing zeroes is often limited by the number of factors of 5.\n\nTo find the number of factors of 5 in $42!$, calculate $\\lfloor 42/5 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 42/25 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 42/125 \\rfloor + \\cdots$.  \n- $\\lfloor 42/5 \\rfloor = 8$ (multiples of 5: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40)  \n- $\\lfloor 42/25 \\rfloor = 1$ (multiples of 25: 25)  \n- $\\lfloor 42/125 \\rfloor = 0$ (since $125 > 42$)  \nThe total is $8 + 1 = 9$.\n\nThe number of factors of 2 in $42!$ is $\\lfloor 42/2 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 42/4 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 42/8 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 42/16 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 42/32 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 42/64 \\rfloor = 21 + 10 + 5 + 2 + 1 + 0 = 39$, which is greater than 9.\n\nThus, the number of trailing zeroes is determined by the number of factors of 5, which is 9.\n\n\\boxed{9}",
