    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem and provide the final answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Define\n\\[p = \\sum_{{k = 1}}^\\infty \\frac{{1}}{{k^2}} \\quad \\text{{and}} \\quad q = \\sum_{{k = 1}}^\\infty \\frac{{1}}{{k^3}}.\\]Find a way to write\n\\[\\sum_{{j = 1}}^\\infty \\sum_{{k = 1}}^\\infty \\frac{{1}}{{(j + k)^3}}\\]in terms of $p$ and $q.$\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\nThe double sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j+k)^3}\\) is evaluated by setting \\(m = j + k\\). For each \\(m \\geq 2\\), the sum over pairs \\((j, k)\\) such that \\(j + k = m\\) gives \\((m-1)\\) terms, each contributing \\(\\frac{1}{m^3}\\), so the sum for each \\(m\\) is \\(\\frac{m-1}{m^3}\\). Thus, the double sum is \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\frac{m-1}{m^3}\\).\n\nSimplifying \\(\\frac{m-1}{m^3}\\) gives \\(\\frac{m-1}{m^3} = \\frac{1}{m^2} - \\frac{1}{m^3}\\). The sum is then \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{m^2} - \\frac{1}{m^3} \\right)\\).\n\nSince \\(p = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^2}\\), \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{m^2} = p - 1\\). Similarly, since \\(q = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}\\), \\(\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{m^3} = q - 1\\). Substituting these gives:\n\\[\n\\sum_{m=2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{m^2} - \\frac{1}{m^3} \\right) = (p - 1) - (q - 1) = p - q.\n\\]\nThe term for \\(m=1\\) in \\(p - q\\) is \\(1 - 1 = 0\\), so \\(p - q = \\sum_{k=2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{k^2} - \\frac{1}{k^3} \\right)\\), which matches the double sum.\n\n\\boxed{p-q}",
