    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem and provide the final answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There are finitely many primes $p$ for which the congruence $$8x\\equiv 1\\pmod{{p}}$$has no solutions $x$. Determine the sum of all such $p$.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\nThe congruence \\(8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}\\) has no solution if and only if \\(\\gcd(8, p)\\) does not divide 1. Since \\(p\\) is prime, \\(\\gcd(8, p)\\) is either 1 or \\(p\\). For \\(\\gcd(8, p)\\) to not divide 1, it must be \\(p\\), and \\(p\\) must divide 8. The prime divisors of 8 are only 2. For \\(p = 2\\), \\(\\gcd(8, 2) = 2\\), which does not divide 1, so there is no solution. For any other prime \\(p\\), \\(\\gcd(8, p) = 1\\), which divides 1, so there is a solution. Thus, the only prime \\(p\\) for which the congruence has no solution is \\(p = 2\\). The sum of all such primes is 2.\n\n\\boxed{2}",
