    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the uncertainty in momentum ($\\Delta p$), we use the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle: $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\frac{\\hbar}{2}$. Given $\\Delta x = 10 \\text{ pm} = 10 \\times 10^{-12} \\text{ m}$ and $\\hbar \\approx 1.055 \\times 10^{-34} \\text{ J}\\cdot\\text{s}$, the minimum uncertainty is $\\Delta p \\approx \\frac{1.055 \\times 10^{-34}}{2 \\times 10^{-11}} \\approx 5.27 \\times 10^{-24} \\text{ kg}\\cdot\\text{m/s}$. However, standard textbook problems often use $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar$ or approximate values leading to slightly different results. Let's check the calculation using $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\approx h / (4\\pi)$ vs just $h$. Using $\\Delta p \\approx \\frac{h}{4\\pi \\Delta x} = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{4\\pi \\times 10^{-11}} \\approx 5.27 \\times 10^{-24}$. This doesn't match the options which are around $10^{-23}$. Let's re-read the question carefully. \"Somewhere in a 10 pm interval\". Often this implies $\\Delta x = 10 \\text{ pm}$. If we assume the inequality is taken as an equality with $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\ge \\hbar/2$, we get $\\sim 5.3 \\times 10^{-24}$. The options are an order of magnitude larger. Let's check if $\\Delta x$ is defined differently or if the constant used is different. Some texts use $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\ge h$. Then $\\Delta p \\approx 6.6 \\times 10^{-24}$. Still small.\n\nLet's look at Option C: $5.0 \\times 10^{-23}$. This is close to $5.27 \\times 10^{-24}$ but off by factor of 10. Did I mess up powers?\n$\\Delta x = 10 \\text{ pm} = 10 \\times 10^{-12} \\text{ m} = 10^{-11} \\text{ m}$.\n$h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$.\n$\\hbar = 1.055 \\times 10^{-34}$.\n$\\Delta p_{min} = \\frac{\\hbar}{2 \\Delta x} = \\frac{1.055 \\times 10^{-34}}{2 \\times 10^{-11}} = 0.5275 \\times 10^{-23} = 5.275 \\times 10^{-24}$.\n\nThe options are all $\\times 10^{-23}$.\nLet's try $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\ge \\hbar$.\n$\\Delta p = \\frac{1.055 \\times 10^{-34}}{10^{-11}} = 1.055 \\times 10^{-23}$. No option matches well.\n\nLet's try $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\ge \\frac{h}{2}$? No, that's not standard.\nWhat if $\\Delta x$ is the full width, so $\\sigma_x$ is half? Or what if the question implies $\\Delta x = 10 \\text{ pm}$ and asks for the value corresponding to $\\Delta p \\approx \\frac{h}{\\Delta x}$?\n$\\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{10^{-11}} = 6.626 \\times 10^{-23}$.\nThis matches Option H: $6.6 \\times 10^{-23}$.\n\nIn many introductory physics contexts, the uncertainty principle is sometimes loosely stated or approximated as $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\approx h$ or similar orders of magnitude estimates to get a quick answer, or specifically using $\\Delta p \\approx h / \\Delta x$ for an order-of-magnitude estimate. Among the choices, $6.6 \\times 10^{-23}$ corresponds exactly to $h / \\Delta x$.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
