    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7663",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: suppose the sequence a_n satisfies $lim_{{n\\rightarrow\\infty}}a_n\\sum_{{i=1}}^n a_i^2=1$. What is the limit of  3n(a_n)^3?\n\nOptions: \nA. 3.0\nB. n^2\nC. -1.0\nD. Undefined\nE. 1.0\nF. 0.0\nG. -Infinity\nH. Infinity\nI. 2.718 (e, the base of natural logarithms)\nJ. 1/n\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let $S_n = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2$. The condition $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n S_n = 1$ implies that $a_n$ must approach 0 and $S_n$ must approach infinity. Assuming $a_n > 0$, we can approximate the difference $S_n - S_{n-1} = a_n^2$. Using the relation $S_n \\approx 1/a_n$, we differentiate to find $dS/dn \\approx -S^2 a' \\approx a^2$. This leads to a differential equation where $a_n \\sim n^{-1/3}$. Specifically, if $a_n = c n^{-1/3}$, then $S_n \\approx \\int c^2 x^{-2/3} dx = 3c^2 n^{1/3}$. Then $a_n S_n \\approx c n^{-1/3} \\cdot 3c^2 n^{1/3} = 3c^3 = 1$, so $c = (1/3)^{1/3}$. We need the limit of $3n a_n^3 = 3n (c n^{-1/3})^3 = 3n c^3 n^{-1} = 3c^3$. Since $3c^3 = 1$, the limit is 1.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
