      "generated_answer": "We need to find where $f''(x) = 0$ for $f(x) = \\frac{10\\ln(x)}{x^2}$.\n\nFirst, $f'(x) = \\frac{10/x \\cdot x^2 - 10\\ln(x) \\cdot 2x}{x^4} = \\frac{10 - 20\\ln(x)}{x^3} = \\frac{10(1 - 2\\ln(x))}{x^3}$.\n\nNext, $f''(x) = \\frac{d}{dx}\\left[10(1-2\\ln(x)) \\cdot x^{-3}\\right]$\n\n$= 10\\left[\\frac{-2}{x} \\cdot x^{-3} + (1-2\\ln(x))(-3x^{-4})\\right]$\n\n$= 10\\left[-2x^{-4} - 3x^{-4}(1-2\\ln(x))\\right]$\n\n$= \\frac{-10}{x^4}\\left[2 + 3(1-2\\ln(x))\\right]$\n\n$= \\frac{-10}{x^4}\\left[5 - 6\\ln(x)\\right]$\n\nSetting $f''(x) = 0$: $5 - 6\\ln(x) = 0$, so $\\ln(x) = 5/6$, giving $x = e^{5/6} \\approx 2.301$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
