    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6473",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose that $f(x)$ is a polynomial that has degree $6$ and $g(x)$ is a polynomial that has degree $3$. If $h(x)$ is also a polynomial such that $f(g(x)) + g(h(x)) + h(f(x))$ is a polynomial of degree $36$, then what is the degree of the polynomial $h$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 3\nB. 21\nC. 6\nD. 9\nE. 30\nF. 72\nG. 12\nH. 15\nI. 24\nJ. 18\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the degree of $h(x)$, let $\\deg(h) = n$. The degrees of the composite terms are: $\\deg(f(g(x))) = \\deg(f)\\cdot\\deg(g) = 6 \\cdot 3 = 18$, $\\deg(g(h(x))) = \\deg(g)\\cdot\\deg(h) = 3n$, and $\\deg(h(f(x))) = \\deg(h)\\cdot\\deg(f) = 6n$. For the sum to have degree 36, the highest degree term must come from one of these components, so we set the maximum of $\\{18, 3n, 6n\\}$ equal to 36. Since $6n > 3n$ for positive $n$, we solve $6n = 36$, which gives $n = 6$. This results in degrees 18, 18, and 36, so the maximum is indeed 36.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
