    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7100",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following are valid intervals of convergence for a power series?\n(I) [−1, 3)\n(II) (−∞, 0]\n(III) [2, 2] ∪ [3, 3]\n(IV) (−∞,∞)\n\nOptions: \nA. Only (III)\nB. Only (II)\nC. (I), (III) and (IV)\nD. All of them\nE. (I) and (IV )\nF. (II) and (IV)\nG. Only (IV )\nH. (II), (III) and (IV)\nI. (I), (II) and (III)\nJ. (II) and (III)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The interval of convergence for a power series centered at $c$ must be an interval symmetric around $c$ (specifically $(c-R, c+R)$, possibly including endpoints). Intervals like $(-\\infty, 0]$ or finite disjoint sets are not valid because they either lack symmetry or are not single connected intervals extending from a center. Only intervals that can be represented as $(c-R, c+R)$ with possible endpoint inclusion, or the entire real line $\\mathbb{R}$, are valid. Therefore, $[-1, 3)$ is invalid (not symmetric around its midpoint 1 unless radius is 2, but interval length is 4, so center 1 implies radius 2 would be $(-1, 3)$; however, the interval starts at -1 which is distance 2 from 1, ends at 3 which is distance 2 from 1. Wait, $[-1, 3)$ has center 1. Distance to -1 is 2. Distance to 3 is 2. So radius is 2. The interval should be $(-1, 3)$ or similar. $[-1, 3)$ includes -1 but excludes 3. This is a valid interval of convergence for a specific series. Let's re-evaluate. A power series $\\sum a_n (x-c)^n$ has an interval of convergence that is an interval centered at $c$. It can be $(c-R, c+R)$, $[c-R, c+R]$, etc.\nLet's check (I) $[-1, 3)$. Center is $(-1+3)/2 = 1$. Radius $R=2$. Interval is $[1-2, 1+2) = [-1, 3)$. This is a valid type of interval (open on right, closed on left).\nLet's check (II) $(-\\infty, 0]$. This is not symmetric around any finite center. Power series converge on intervals symmetric around the center $c$. If $R=\\infty$, it is $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. If $R$ is finite, it is bounded. So $(-\\infty, 0]$ is impossible.\nLet's check (III) $[2, 2] \\cup [3, 3]$. This is not a connected interval. Convergence intervals must be connected. Invalid.\nLet's check (IV) $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. This corresponds to $R=\\infty$. Valid.\n\nSo valid are (I) and (IV).\n\n\\boxed{E}",
