    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the standard Gibbs energy of formation of $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}(aq)$, we use the relationship $\\Delta G^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ$ for the half-reaction $\\mathrm{AgCl}(s) + e^- \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ag}(s) + \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}(aq)$. The standard Gibbs energy change for this reaction is $\\Delta G^\\circ = \\Delta_f G^\\circ(\\mathrm{Ag}) + \\Delta_f G^\\circ(\\mathrm{Cl}^-) - \\Delta_f G^\\circ(\\mathrm{AgCl})$. Since $\\Delta_f G^\\circ(\\mathrm{Ag}) = 0$, we have $\\Delta_f G^\\circ(\\mathrm{Cl}^-) = \\Delta G^\\circ + \\Delta_f G^\\circ(\\mathrm{AgCl})$. Calculating $\\Delta G^\\circ = -(1)(96485 \\text{ C/mol})(0.222 \\text{ V}) \\approx -21.42 \\text{ kJ/mol}$, then $\\Delta_f G^\\circ(\\mathrm{Cl}^-) = -21.42 \\text{ kJ/mol} + (-109.71 \\text{ kJ/mol}) = -131.13 \\text{ kJ/mol}$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
