    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10350",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A single phase one pulse controlled circuit has a resistance R and counter emf E load 400 sin(314 t) as the source voltage. For a load counter emf of 200 V, the range of firing angle control is\n\nOptions: \nA. 60° to 180°.\nB. 90° to 150°.\nC. 60° to 150°.\nD. 30° to 120°.\nE. 30° to 180°.\nF. 30° to 150°.\nG. 60° to 120°.\nH. 90° to 180°.\nI. 45° to 180°.\nJ. 45° to 135°.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the range of the firing angle $\\alpha$, we first calculate the minimum angle required to overcome the counter EMF ($E$). The source voltage is $V_m = 400$ V and $E = 200$ V. Conduction can only start when $V_s \\ge E$, so $\\sin(\\alpha_{min}) = E/V_m = 200/400 = 0.5$, which gives $\\alpha_{min} = 30^\\circ$. The maximum firing angle for a resistive load with counter EMF in a half-wave or full-wave controlled rectifier context (assuming single pulse implies half-wave or specific conduction window) typically extends until the voltage drops below E again or reaches the end of the positive half-cycle. For a single-phase one-pulse circuit (half-wave), conduction stops when the current becomes zero. However, the question asks for the *range of firing angle control*. The thyristor must be fired after $\\alpha_{min}$ to allow current flow. The upper limit is determined by the point where the voltage falls back to $E$ or $180^\\circ$. The voltage equals $E$ at $\\pi - \\alpha_{min} = 150^\\circ$. Firing after $150^\\circ$ means the voltage never exceeds $E$ in that half cycle, so no conduction occurs. Thus, the controllable range is from $30^\\circ$ to $150^\\circ$.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
