      "generated_answer": "The Nyquist rate for a 5 kHz signal is $2 \\times 5 \\text{ kHz} = 10 \\text{ kHz}$, so the sampling frequency $f_s$ is 10,000 samples/sec per channel. With 2,000 signals time-division multiplexed, the total sampling rate is $2000 \\times 10,000 = 2 \\times 10^7$ Hz, making the sampling time $T_s = 1/f_{total} = 0.5 \\times 10^{-7}$ sec. However, looking at the options, Option E suggests $T_s = 1 \\times 10^{-4}$ sec which corresponds to a much lower total rate, or perhaps the question implies $T_s$ is the period of one sample slot in the TDM frame. Let's re-read carefully: \"sampled at Nyquist rate\". $f_s = 10$ kHz. Period $T = 1/10kHz = 10^{-4}$ sec. This is the time between samples for *one* signal. In TDM with 2000 signals, the pulse duration $\\tau$ must be less than the time slot allocated to each signal. The time slot duration is $T_{slot} = T / N = 10^{-4} / 2000 = 0.5 \\times 10^{-7}$ sec. Thus $\\tau_{max} = 0.5 \\times 10^{-7}$ sec. The sampling time $T_s$ usually refers to the inverse of the Nyquist rate for the individual signal, which is $1/(2 \\times 5000) = 10^{-4}$ sec. Therefore, $T_s = 1 \\times 10^{-4}$ sec and $\\tau_{max} = 0.5 \\times 10^{-7}$ sec.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
