    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10927",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A television picture is composed of approximately 300,000 individual picture elements, each of which can attain ten different and distinguishable brightness levels (such as black and shades of gray) for proper contrast. If, for any picture element, the probability of occurrence of the ten brightness levels are equal, and 30 picture frames are being transmitted per second, determine the bandwidth of the T.V. video signal. Assume that the signal-to-noise ratio of 1000 (30 db) is required for proper reproduction of any picture.\n\nOptions: \nA. 2.5MHz\nB. 1.5MHz\nC. 6MHz\nD. 8MHz\nE. 2MHz\nF. 4MHz\nG. 3MHz\nH. 1MHz\nI. 7MHz\nJ. 5MHz\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the bandwidth of a TV video signal.\n\n**Step 1: Information per picture element**\n\nWith 10 equally likely brightness levels:\n$$H = \\log_2(10) \\approx 3.32 \\text{ bits/pixel}$$\n\n**Step 2: Information per picture frame**\n\nFor 300,000 picture elements:\n$$H_{frame} = 300,000 \\times 3.32 = 996,000 \\text{ bits/frame}$$\n\n**Step 3: Information rate (channel capacity needed)**\n\nFor 30 frames per second:\n$$R = 996,000 \\times 30 = 29.88 \\times 10^6 \\text{ bits/second}$$\n\n**Step 4: Apply Shannon-Hartley theorem**\n\n$$C = B \\log_2(1 + S/N)$$\n\nWith S/N = 1000:\n$$B = \\frac{R}{\\log_2(1 + 1000)} = \\frac{29.88 \\times 10^6}{\\log_2(1001)} = \\frac{29.88 \\times 10^6}{9.97} \\approx 3 \\text{ MHz}$$\n\n\\boxed{G}",
