    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10237",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Design a square key for a 3 in. diameter shaft to withstand a torque of 30,000 lbs.-in. The design stresses in shear and bearing can be safely taken as 11,000 psi and 22,000 psi, respectively.\n\nOptions: \nA. thickness = 1/2 in., width = 1/2 in., length = 3 in.\nB. thickness = 3/4 in., width = 3/4 in., length = 2.5 in.\nC. thickness = 1 in., width = 1 in., length = 2 in.\nD. thickness = 1/2 in., width = 3/4 in., length = 2.5 in.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct square key dimensions, we first calculate the required width and thickness based on the shaft diameter and standard proportions (typically $w = t = D/4$). For a 3-inch shaft, $w = t = 3/4$ inch. Next, we verify the length required to withstand the torque using the shear stress formula $\\tau = \\frac{2T}{D \\cdot L \\cdot w}$ or bearing stress formula $\\sigma_b = \\frac{4T}{D \\cdot L \\cdot t}$. Using the shear stress limit of 11,000 psi, the required length is approximately $L = \\frac{2(30,000)}{3(2.5)(0.75)} \\approx 2.67$ inches (using half depth for shear area approximation) or more accurately $L = \\frac{T}{\\tau \\cdot A_s}$. Let's use the standard design check: Torque $T = F \\cdot (D/2)$, so Force $F = 20,000$ lbs. Shear Area $A_s = L \\cdot w$. $\\tau = F / A_s \\Rightarrow 11,000 = 20,000 / (L \\cdot 0.75) \\Rightarrow L = 20,000 / (11,000 \\cdot 0.75) \\approx 2.42$ inches. Bearing Area $A_b = L \\cdot (t/2)$. $\\sigma_b = F / A_b \\Rightarrow 22,000 = 20,000 / (L \\cdot 0.375) \\Rightarrow L = 20,000 / (22,000 \\cdot 0.375) \\approx 2.42$ inches. Option B has a length of 2.5 inches, which satisfies both conditions ($2.5 > 2.42$). Option A has incorrect dimensions for a 3-inch shaft. Option C is too thick/wide. Option D is not square.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
