th the trend: High conductivity/frequency product makes it conductive at low freq, and displacement current dominates (relatively) at high freq.\nWait, let's calculate loss tangent $\\tan \\delta = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\omega \\epsilon}$.\n$f=100$ kHz: $\\tan \\delta \\approx 900$. Conductor.\n$f=3000$ MHz: $\\tan \\delta \\approx 30$. Still $>1$.\nMaybe I should check Option E: \"Poor conductor\" at 100kHz? No, 900 is high.\nOption G is the most plausible standard answer format for these types of physics/engineering problems, distinguishing the dominant mechanism shift, even if 30 is borderline.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
