      "generated_answer": "To find the initial value of $f(t) = e^{-2t}u(t)$, we evaluate $\\lim_{t \\to 0^+} f(t) = e^0 = 1$. The derivative is $f'(t) = -2e^{-2t}u(t)$, so the initial slope is $\\lim_{t \\to 0^+} f'(t) = -2$. Alternatively, using the Initial Value Theorem on $F(s) = \\frac{1}{s+2}$, $\\lim_{s \\to \\infty} sF(s) = 1$, and for the slope, $\\lim_{s \\to \\infty} s[sF(s) - f(0)] = \\lim_{s \\to \\infty} s(\\frac{s}{s+2} - 1) = -2$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
