    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the Fourier transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t} \\sin(6t - 18) u(t - 3)$, we first simplify the sine term using the identity $\\sin(A-B)$. Since $\\sin(6t - 18) = \\sin(6(t-3))$, the function becomes $f(t) = e^{-2t} \\sin(6(t-3)) u(t-3)$.\nWe rewrite the exponential term to match the shift in time: $e^{-2t} = e^{-2(t-3+3)} = e^{-6} e^{-2(t-3)}$. Thus, $f(t) = e^{-6} [e^{-2(t-3)} \\sin(6(t-3)) u(t-3)]$.\nLet $g(t) = e^{-2t} \\sin(6t) u(t)$. The Fourier transform of $g(t)$ is given by the formula with $\\alpha=2$ and $\\omega_0=6$:\n$$ G(j\\omega) = \\frac{6}{(2+j\\omega)^2 + 36} $$\nUsing the time-shifting property $\\mathcal{F}\\{g(t-t_0)\\} = e^{-j\\omega t_0} G(j\\omega)$ with $t_0=3$, the transform of the shifted part is $e^{-3j\\omega} G(j\\omega)$.\nIncluding the constant factor $e^{-6}$, the total transform is:\n$$ F(j\\omega) = e^{-6} e^{-3j\\omega} \\frac{6}{(j\\omega+2)^2 + 36} = \\frac{6 e^{-6} e^{-3j\\omega}}{(j\\omega+2)^2 + 36} $$\nThis matches Option F.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
