      "generated_answer": "To find the short circuit level (MVA) at the busbar, we first calculate the total equivalent subtransient reactance of the four identical alternators connected in parallel. Since they are identical and in parallel, the total MVA capacity is $4 \\times 20 \\text{ MVA} = 80 \\text{ MVA}$, and the equivalent percentage reactance is $16\\% / 4 = 4\\%$. The short circuit MVA is calculated as $\\frac{\\text{Base MVA}}{\\text{Equivalent \\% Reactance}} \\times 100$, which gives $\\frac{80}{4} \\times 100 = 2000 \\text{ MVA}$? Wait, let's re-evaluate standard formula: Short Circuit MVA = Total MVA / (Equivalent p.u. Reactance). Equivalent p.u. reactance $X_{eq} = 0.16 / 4 = 0.04$. Total MVA base is usually taken as the sum of ratings if calculating from individual bases, but here we can just use the formula: $SC MVA = \\frac{\\text{Total MVA Rating}}{\\text{Equivalent pu Reactance}}$. Total MVA = 80 MVA. $X_{pu} = 0.04$. $SC MVA = 80 / 0.04 = 2000$ MVA. This option is not listed. Let's re-read carefully. \"Four identical alternators... rated for 20 MVA... reactance 16%\". Maybe the question implies a different base or calculation method often found in textbooks.\n\nLet's try calculating the short circuit current first or using the formula $SC MVA = \\frac{MVA_{total}}{X_{eq\\_pu}}$.\nIf we consider the system base as 20 MVA (one unit):\n$X_{eq} = 0.16 / 4 = 0.04$ pu.\n$SC MVA = 20 / 0.04 = 500$ MVA.\nThis matches Option D.\n\nLet's verify.\nShort Circuit MVA = $\\frac{\\text{Base MVA}}{\\text{Equivalent Reactance in pu}}$.\nIf we choose Base MVA = 20 MVA (rating of one generator).\nEach generator has $X = 0.16$ pu on its own base.\nFour in parallel: $X_{eq} = 0.16 / 4 = 0.04$ pu on 20 MVA base.\n$SC MVA = 20 / 0.04 = 500$ MVA.\n\nAlternatively, if Base MVA = 80 MVA.\n$X_{gen} = 0.16 \\times (80/20) = 0.64$ pu on new base.\n$X_{eq} = 0.64 / 4 = 0.16$ pu.\n$SC MVA = 80 / 0.16 = 500$ MVA.\n\nThe result is consistently 500 MVA.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
