    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10292",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A long cylindrical log of specific gravity (2/3) will float in water with its axis horizontal. As its length gradually is reduced it will float in the same position. If its length is reduced so that it is only a fraction of its diameter, it will float with its axis vertical. What is its diairieter-to-length ratio for which it will just float with its axis vertical?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.5\nB. 2.5\nC. 1.33\nD. 0.8\nE. 2.0\nF. 0.5\nG. 1.1\nH. 1.75\nI. 1.0\nJ. 1.25\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the stability limit, we compare the metacentric height ($GM$) for vertical orientation. For a cylinder floating vertically, stability requires $GM > 0$, which leads to the condition $\\frac{\\pi D^4}{64 V_{sub}} > BG$. With specific gravity $SG = 2/3$, the submerged depth is $h = SG \\cdot L = \\frac{2}{3}L$. The center of buoyancy $B$ is at $h/2 = L/3$ from the bottom, and the center of gravity $G$ is at $L/2$ from the bottom, so $BG = L/6$. The second moment of area $I = \\pi D^4 / 64$ and submerged volume $V_{sub} = \\frac{\\pi D^2}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2L}{3} = \\frac{\\pi D^2 L}{6}$. The condition $\\frac{I}{V_{sub}} > BG$ becomes $\\frac{\\pi D^4 / 64}{\\pi D^2 L / 6} > L/6$, which simplifies to $\\frac{3 D^2}{32 L} > \\frac{L}{6}$, or $\\frac{D^2}{L^2} > \\frac{32}{18} = \\frac{16}{9}$. Thus, $\\frac{D}{L} > \\frac{4}{3} \\approx 1.33$. The critical ratio is $1.33$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
