    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The electric field is polarized in the $\\hat{j}$ direction and propagates in the $+\\hat{k}$ (z) direction. In a plane wave in free space, the magnetic field intensity $\\mathbf{H}$ is related to the electric field $\\mathbf{E}$ by $\\mathbf{H} = \\frac{1}{\\eta} (\\hat{k} \\times \\mathbf{E})$, where $\\eta = \\sqrt{\\mu_0/\\epsilon_0} = c\\mu_0$. Since $\\hat{k} \\times \\hat{j} = -\\hat{i}$, the magnetic field is polarized in the $-\\hat{i}$ direction and has the same phase as the electric field. Therefore, $\\mathbf{H} = -\\frac{A}{c\\mu_0} \\cos(\\omega(t - z/c)) \\hat{i}$.\n\nLooking at the options, Option G matches this form: $H= - {{iA/ c\\mu_0}}sin\\omega {{t - (z/c)}}$? No, wait. The electric field is cosine, so the magnetic field should also be cosine. Let's re-evaluate the cross product and impedance.\n$\\mathbf{E} = E_0 \\cos(\\omega t - kz) \\hat{j}$.\n$\\mathbf{H} = \\frac{E_0}{\\eta} \\cos(\\omega t - kz) (\\hat{k} \\times \\hat{j}) = \\frac{E_0}{\\eta} \\cos(\\omega t - kz) (-\\hat{i})$.\n$\\eta = c \\mu_0$. So $\\mathbf{H} = -\\frac{A}{c \\mu_0} \\cos(\\omega t - k z) \\hat{i}$.\n\nLet's check the options again.\nA. Sine, wrong phase.\nB. Positive i, wrong direction.\nC. Positive k, wrong direction.\nD. Sine, wrong phase.\nE. Double cos, typo? And negative i. But it says coscos. Likely a typo for just cos.\nF. Positive j, wrong polarization.\nG. Sine, wrong phase.\nH. Double cos.\nI. Sine.\nJ. Wrong units/factor.\n\nWait, let's look closer at the question text. \"Determine the time-dependent magnetic intensity H\".\nUsually, for a wave traveling in +z, if E is along y ($\\hat{j}$), H is along x ($\\hat{i}$).\nDirection: $\\hat{k}_{prop} \\times \\hat{E} = \\hat{H}_{dir}$.\n$\\hat{z} \\times \\hat{y} = -\\hat{x}$. So H is in $-\\hat{i}$ direction.\nMagnitude: $H_0 = E_0 / \\eta_0 = A / (c \\mu_0)$.\nPhase: Same as E, which is cosine.\nSo $\\mathbf{H} = -\\frac{A}{c \\mu_0} \\cos(\\omega(t - z/c)) \\hat{i}$.\n\nNone of the options perfectly match \"$-\\hat{i} \\frac{A}{c \\mu_0} \\cos(...)$\".\nLet's re-read Option E: `H= - {{iA/ c\\mu_0}}coscos\\omega {{t - (z/c)}}`. This looks like a typo for `cos`.\nLet's re-read Option B: `H= {{iA/ c\\mu_0}}cos...`. Wrong sign.\nLet's re-read Option G: `H= - {{iA/ c\\mu_0}}sin...`. Wrong function.\n\nIs it possible the question implies a different coordinate system or definition?\nOr perhaps I made a mistake in $\\eta$?\n$\\eta_0 = \\sqrt{\\mu_0/\\epsilon_0}$.\n$c = 1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0 \\epsilon_0} \\implies \\epsilon_0 = 1/(\\mu_0 c^2)$.\n$\\eta_0 = \\sqrt{\\mu_0 / (1/(\\mu_0 c^2))} = \\sqrt{\\mu_0^2 c^2} = c \\mu_0$. Correct.\n\nLet's look at Option E again. It has `-i`, magnitude `A/c\\mu_0`, and `cos` (despite the typo `coscos`). This is the only option with the correct vector direction ($-\\hat{i}$) and correct functional form (cosine), assuming the double word is a typo.\n\nHowever, let's look at Option A, D, G, I. They use sine. Maxwell's equation $\\nabla \\times E = -\\mu_0 \\partial H / \\partial t$.\nIf $E_y = A \\cos(\\omega t - kz)$, then $\\frac{\\partial E_y}{\\partial z} = A k \\sin(\\omega t - kz)$.\n$(\\nabla \\times E)_x = -\\frac{\\partial E_y}{\\partial z} = -Ak \\sin(\\omega t - kz)$.\n$-\\mu_0 \\frac{\\partial H_x}{\\partial t} = -Ak \\sin(\\omega t - kz)$.\n$\\frac{\\partial H_x}{\\partial t} = \\frac{Ak}{\\mu_0} \\sin(\\omega t - kz)$.\nIntegrating with respect to t:\n$H_x = \\frac{Ak}{\\mu_0} \\int \\sin(\\omega t - kz) dt = \\frac{Ak}{\\mu_0} \\left( -\\frac{1}{\\omega} \\cos(\\omega t - kz) \\right)$.\nSince $k/\\omega = 1/c$,\n$H_x = -\\frac{A}{c \\mu_0} \\cos(\\omega t - kz)$.\nThis confirms the cosine dependence and the negative sign.\n\nTherefore, the answer must be the one with $-\\hat{i}$ and $\\cos$.\nOption E is `H= - {{iA/ c\\mu_0}}coscos\\omega {{t - (z/c)}}`.\nOption H is `H= {{iA/ c\\mu_0}}coscos\\omega {{t - (z/c)}}`.\n\nOption E has the correct negative sign for the i-component. The \"coscos\" is almost certainly a typo for \"cos\".\n\n\\boxed{E}",
