et's consider the definition of rate constants. Often $k_G$ is given in mol/(m² s Pa). If pressure increases, the concentration gradient in terms of mole fraction might change differently.\nActually, there is a known result for this specific problem found in chemical engineering question banks (e.g., from McCabe-Smith or similar unit operations quizzes).\nCase I: Methane in Air, Pure Water, Gas Film Controlled.\nDriving force $\\Delta p = p_{bulk} - p_{interface}$. $p_{interface} \\approx 0$.\nRate $\\propto p_{bulk} = y P$.\nIf we go from 1 atm to 3 atm, $p_{bulk}$ goes from $0.15 \\times 1 = 0.15$ to $0.15 \\times 3 = 0.45$.\nThe rate should triple (increase by 200%). None of the options say 200%.\n\nLet's reconsider the mass transfer coefficient dependence. $k_G \\propto D / RT \\delta$. It is independent of P in terms of partial pressure driving force? No, $N_A = k_G (p_1 - p_2)$. If $k_G$ is constant, rate triples.\nMaybe the question implies liquid film control? No, it says \"gas film offers the main resistance\".\n\nLet's look at Case II. Solvent has vapor pressure 0.17 atm.\n$p_{interface} = 0.17$ atm (assuming equilibrium with the solvent surface).\nAt 1 atm: $p_{bulk} = 0.25$ atm. Driving force $\\Delta p_1 = 0.25 - 0.17 = 0.08$ atm.\nAt 3 atm: $p_{bulk} = 0.25 \\times 3 = 0.75$ atm. Driving force $\\Delta p_2 = 0.75 - 0.17 = 0.58$ atm.\nRate ratio = $0.58 / 0.08 = 7.25$. Huge increase.\n\nThis suggests my interpretation of \"vapor pressure of methane over the solution\" might mean the partial pressure of methane in equilibrium with the *initial* solvent concentration. If the solvent is a solution of CH4, and we assume the bulk liquid concentration doesn't change instantly, the interfacial partial pressure is fixed at 0.17 atm.\n\nLet's check the options again.\nOption A: 1st case +10%, 2nd case no effect.\nOption B: Increases in both.\nOption C: No effect 1st, decreases 2nd.\nOption J: No effect 1st, 126% gain 2nd.\n\nIs it possible that for Case I, the i