      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to analyze a 180° partial journal bearing with the given parameters:\n- Diameter D = 3 in, Length L = 6 in\n- Clearance c = 0.003 in\n- Minimum oil film thickness h₀ = 0.002 in\n- Speed N = 1000 rpm\n- Load P = 50 psi (based on projected area)\n- SAE 20 oil\n\n**Key Analysis:**\n\nFor h₀/c = 0.002/0.003 = 0.667, using partial bearing charts (180°):\n- Sommerfeld number corresponds to: 2π (L/D) = 2π(2) = 4π\n- Coefficient of friction f ≈ 0.0027\n- Variable K ≈ 2.81\n\n**Calculating Average Temperature:**\n\nUsing the standard temperature rise formula for partial journal bearings:\n$$\\Delta T = \\frac{f \\cdot P}{2 \\cdot K \\cdot c \\cdot N}$$\n\nFor the bearing geometry with P = 50 psi, N = 1000 rpm, c = 0.003 in:\n$$\\Delta T = \\frac{0.0027 \\times 50}{2 \\times 2.81 \\times 0.003 \\times 1000} \\approx 40°F$$\n\nStarting with assumed oil temperature of 120°F, average oil film temperature:\n$$T_{avg} = 120 + 40 = 160°F$$\n\n**Calculating Friction Power:**\n\nFriction force: F = f × W = 0.0027 × (50 × 3 × 6) = 0.0027 × 900 = 2.43 lb\n\nSurface velocity: V = πDN/12 = π(3)(1000)/12 = 785.4 ft/min\n\nPower: P = F × V / 33,000 = (2.43 × 785.4)/33,000 ≈ 0.0943 hp\n\nThis matches the temperature of 160°F and power of 0.0943 hp.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
