    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5408",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which statement about intestinal \"brakes\" and \"accelerators\" is FALSE?\n\n\nOptions: \nA. Short-chain fatty acids which move from colon to caecum (reflux) promote ileo-caecal motility\nB. The arrival of fiber in the colon causes an increase in motility and a switch from the \"fasting\" to the \"feeding\" pattern.\nC. The arrival of lipid in the duodenum causes an immediate reduction in motility and a switch from the \"fasting\" to the \"feeding\" pattern.\nD. Food in the stomach causes gastrin release, which stimulates acid secretion\nE. Protein ingestion leads to immediate increase in intestinal motility\nF. Gastric acid secretion inhibits small intestinal motility\nG. The presence of bile in the intestine slows down motility\nH. The presence of undigested food in the colon stimulates intestinal motility\nI. Dietary starch is a potent stimulator of small intestinal motility because it causes insulin release from the pancreas\nJ. The presence of glucose in the duodenum accelerates intestinal motility\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **I**.\n\nInsulin release does not directly stimulate small intestinal motility; in fact, high glucose loads in the duodenum typically trigger the enterogastric reflex to slow gastric emptying and intestinal transit, rather than accelerating it. Furthermore, dietary starch must be digested into glucose before absorption, so undigested starch reaching the small intestine is not the primary mechanism for such a response, and insulin itself is not a direct motility stimulator.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
