      "generated_answer": "Looking at this clinical scenario, I need to consider the next step for a patient with pathologic nipple discharge (black, spontaneous, unilateral).\n\n**Pathologic nipple discharge** characteristics include spontaneous, unilateral, single-duct, and bloody/serous (non-milky) discharge. Black discharge is concerning and warrants further evaluation to rule out intraductal pathology such as papilloma or ductal carcinoma.\n\n**Recommended workup** for pathologic nipple discharge involves imaging first (diagnostic mammography and ultrasound) to identify any underlying lesion. However, since she had recent mammography and ultrasound 2 months ago showing benign findings, and the discharge is the new concerning symptom, **MRI of the breast** is the most appropriate next step as it has higher sensitivity for detecting occult intraductal lesions and malignancies that may have been missed on conventional imaging. If MRI is negative or identifies a lesion, ductography or surgical excision may follow.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
