    "global index": "MedMCQA_720",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 60-year-old has got severe bulla and target lesion which erythema around halo and genital lesions:\n\nOptions: \nA. Stevens Jhonson syndrome\nB. Herpes zoster\nC. Herpes simplex\nD. Herpangina\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Based on the clinical presentation described—severe bullae, target lesions with erythema around a halo, and genital lesions in a 60-year-old—the most likely diagnosis is **Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)**. SJS is a severe mucocutaneous reaction often triggered by medications, characterized by target lesions and involvement of mucous membranes including the genitals. Herpes zoster and simplex typically present with grouped vesicles in a dermatomal or localized distribution, while herpangina primarily affects the oropharynx without skin target lesions.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
