tive?\nLet's use the property: Interval + Inversion = 9 (generic).\nQuality: Augmented <-> Diminished.\nSo Double Augmented <-> Double Diminished.\nInterval: Octave <-> Unison.\nSo Double Augmented Octave <-> Double Diminished Unison.\nA Double Diminished Unison consists of the same note name lowered by two semitones (e.g., C to Bbb). This sounds like a Major Seventh (C to Bb is minor seventh, C to B is major seventh... wait. C down to Bbb is a diminished second? No. C up to Bbb is a diminished unison? No, C up to Bbb is a diminished second? No, C to B is a major seventh. C to Bb is a minor seventh. C to Bbb is a diminished seventh? No.\nLet's stick to the generic inversion.\nOctave (8) inverts to Unison (1).\nAugmented (A) inverts to Diminished (d).\nTherefore, the answer must be a type of Diminished Unison.\nOption A is \"Diminished unison\".\nOption C is \"Augmented unison\".\nSince the quality inverts to Diminished, A is the correct choice among the provided options, assuming the \"double\" aspect is either ignored in the distractors or \"Diminished unison\" is the intended category.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
