    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements about chords are correct? ( )\n① There are 2 common chords between C major harmony and c natural minor harmony\n② The Neapolitan sixth chords in C major and c minor are identical\n③ The chords adjacent to the Neapolitan sixth chord are composed of minor third and perfect fourth intervals\n\nOptions: \nA. 0\nB. 1\nC. 2\nD. 3\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Statement ① is incorrect because C major and c natural minor share only 4 diatonic chords (I, ii°, III, IV, V, vi, vii° vs i, ii°, III+, iv, v, VI, VII), specifically I (C), ii° (Dm7b5) is not shared, wait: C Major: C, Dm, Em, F, G, Am, Bdim. C Minor: Cm, Ddim, Eb, Fm, G, Ab, Bb. Shared chords are F (IV in major, iv in minor). Actually, let's re-evaluate. C Major: C, Dm, Em, F, G, Am, Bdim. C Natural Minor: Cm, Ddim, Eb, Fm, G, Am, Bb. The only common chord is A minor (vi in major, vi in minor)? No, G major is V in major, G minor is v in natural minor. So G major vs G minor. They are different. Let's look closer. C Major: C-E-G, D-F-A, E-G-B, F-A-C, G-B-D, A-C-E, B-D-F. C Natural Minor: C-Eb-G, D-F-Ab, Eb-G-Bb, F-Ab-C, G-Bb-D, Ab-C-Eb, Bb-D-F. Common chords: None of the triads are identical except maybe if we consider quality? No, \"chords\" usually implies specific triads. Wait, A-C-E is in both? In C major, A-C-E is vi. In C natural minor, A-C-E is... A is the 6th degree of C minor? No, A is not in C natural minor scale (Ab is). So A-C-E is not in C natural minor. What about F-A-C? In C major, it's IV. In C natural minor, F-A-C is not diatonic (F-Ab-C is iv). So actually, there are **zero** common diatonic triads between C Major and C Natural Minor. Thus ① is false.\n\nStatement ②: The Neapolitan sixth in C major is built on the flattened second degree (Db). The chord is Db-F-Ab. In first inversion (sixth), it is F-Ab-Db. In c minor, the Neapolitan sixth is also built on Db (the lowered second degree of the harmonic/melodic minor context, or just the key signature adjustment). The chord is Db-F-Ab. In first inversion, it is F-Ab-Db. They are identical. Thus ② is true.\n\nStatement ③: The Neapolitan sixth chord (N6) typically resolves to the dominant (V) or cadential 6/4. The interval between the root of the N6 (Db) and the root of the V (G) is an augmented fourth/diminished fifth. The statement says \"chords adjacent to the Neapolitan sixth chord are composed of minor third and perfect fourth intervals\". This phrasing is ambiguous. It might refer to the intervals *within* the chord? N6 is a major triad in first inversion. Intervals: F to Ab is minor third, Ab to Db is perfect fourth. Or F to Db is perfect fourth. So the chord itself is composed of these intervals. If \"adjacent\" means the chord itself (as it sits next to other chords in progression), then yes, the N6 triad consists of a minor third and a perfect fourth stacked. Thus ③ is likely considered true in this context.\n\nSo, ② and ③ are correct. That makes 2 correct statements.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
