    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4699",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Research on C3 and C4 pathways indicates that changes in plant communities were associated with a transition in the diets of ancient hominids from:\n\nOptions: \nA. meat to fruits and vegetables.\nB. species focused on grasses to fruits and vegetables.\nC. nuts and fruits to fish.\nD. nuts and fruits to species more focused on grasses.\nE. species focused on grasses to meat.\nF. fruits and vegetables to fish.\nG. species focused on grasses to nuts and fruits.\nH. fruits and vegetables to species more focused on grasses.\nI. meat to species more focused on grasses.\nJ. fish to fruits and vegetables.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this question, I need to connect C3 and C4 plant pathways to hominid dietary transitions.\n\nC3 plants (trees, shrubs, nuts, fruits) dominated in earlier, more forested environments, while C4 plants are primarily tropical grasses. Isotopic studies of hominid fossils show a shift from C3 to C4 signatures in tooth enamel over time, indicating that early hominids transitioned from consuming C3-based foods (nuts, fruits) to C4-based foods (grasses and grass-eating animals).\n\nThis research supports the dietary shift from nuts and fruits to grasses/grass-fed animals.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
